On Dec 1, 9:05 pm, Hendrik Weimer <hend...@enyo.de> wrote:

> Jackals are a regular floating eye partner and they can also be summoned
> in large groups by werejackals. So it's not totally unexpected for them
> to be a major problem, especially for inexperienced (or careless)
> players.

I've learned how to deal with floating eyes:  they're slow but will
pursue
you if you're close, so you can easily lure them off the projectiles
you
just threw at them, pick them up and throw them again.  In this manner
one can kill a floating eye with as few as three daggers and no skill.

However, there's another thing I neglected to say about jackals:  they
occur starting on dungeon level one, when your max hitpoints can be
as low as 10.  At that point, virtually anything (that you can't
consistently run circles around) can kill you, especially if you lose
hitpoints fighting something else first.

Owlbears don't appear until somewhere around Delphi (or in the
Mines if you go there first).  A disproportionately high percentage
of deaths occur before this point, even for relatively careful
players.

As the other guy said, they're only scary for a little while, when
you first start to encounter them.  A handful of levels later they're
not such a big deal.  That's true of almost everything, really.  I
mean, in the late game when you have -50 AC and MC3 and
reflection and MR and all the other resistances and 600+ HP
and a couple of blessed unicorn horns and a +7 artifact weapon
or three and every kind of wand you want and a collection of rings
and amulets, and twenty-some-odd potions of holy water, the only
things that can possibly challenge you even a little are unique
named monsters.


